        "The body is built in levels: chemicals assemble into cells, cells of the same kind into tissues, tissues into organs, organs into systems, and systems into one organism. A red blood cell is a cell; blood is a tissue; the heart is an organ; the cardiovascular system is an organ system. Each level performs jobs none of its parts could do alone, and in {S} you learn to place any structure at its correct level before predicting what failure there does to the next.",
        "The cell is the basic living unit. The nucleus holds the DNA and directs the cell; the cytoplasm is a watery gel where the work happens; organelles divide the labour. Mitochondria release energy as ATP, ribosomes build proteins, the endoplasmic reticulum shuttles them, the Golgi apparatus packages and addresses them, and lysosomes digest worn-out material. Human cells are eukaryotic, meaning the genetic material sits inside a true nucleus, the first definition every student of {S} writes down.",
        "The cell membrane is a phospholipid bilayer with proteins floating in it, the fluid mosaic model. Lipid-soluble substances slip through directly; water and ions pass through protein channels or are pumped. Diffusion moves oxygen and carbon dioxide down their gradients, osmosis drags water toward the saltier solution, and active transport spends ATP to move substances against the gradient, as the sodium-potassium pump does every second of life. Transport across this barrier is a standing examination topic in {S}.",
        "Epithelial tissue covers surfaces and lines cavities: the skin epidermis, the gut lining, the airways, and every gland. Its cells sit packed on a basement membrane with almost no matrix, specialised for protection, secretion or absorption. Connective tissue supports and binds, with scattered cells in abundant matrix: bone, cartilage, fat, tendon, and blood, whose liquid plasma is the matrix. The type of epithelium on any surface tells the histologist what wear that surface endures, a reading skill central to {S}.",
        "Muscle tissue contracts and generates movement. Skeletal muscle is striated and voluntary, moving bones on command; cardiac muscle is striated, involuntary and branched, found only in the heart wall; smooth muscle is unstriated and involuntary, lining the gut, blood vessels and uterus. Nervous tissue consists of neurones that transmit impulses and glial cells that support them. In {S}, you learn to tell the three muscles apart by striping, control and location, since one tissue converts chemical energy into mechanical work, the other conducts electricity as its living language.",
