        "The heart is a fist-sized muscular pump with four chambers: right and left atria above, right and left ventricles below. Blood from the body enters the right atrium through the venae cavae, crosses the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle, and is ejected through the pulmonary valve to the lungs. Oxygenated blood returns to the left atrium, crosses the mitral valve into the left ventricle, and leaves through the aortic valve to the whole body. Valves enforce one-way flow, and their closure produces the heart sounds, the pathway every student of {S} recites until it is automatic.",
        "One cardiac cycle is one full heartbeat, about 0.8 seconds at rest. Systole contracts the ventricles and ejects blood; diastole relaxes them and lets them refill. The first heart sound, the lub, is closure of the atrioventricular valves; the second, the dub, is closure of the aortic and pulmonary valves. Cardiac output is stroke volume times heart rate: 70 mL times 72 beats a minute is about 5 litres a minute, the entire blood volume passing the heart each minute at rest, a calculation drilled from the first week of {S}.",
        "Excitation starts in the sinoatrial node, the pacemaker in the right atrial wall that fires 60 to 100 times a minute at rest. The wave spreads across the atria, pauses briefly at the atrioventricular node, then races down the bundle of His, the right and left bundle branches and the Purkinje fibres, contracting the ventricles from the apex upward. The rhythm is built in; the vagus nerve slows it, adrenaline speeds it, and the pause at the node lets atria finish emptying, a sequence central to {S}.",
        "Arteries carry blood away from the heart and have thick elastic walls that smooth each pulse into steady flow. Veins return it, with thinner walls, one-way valves and about two thirds of the blood volume resting in them at any moment. Capillaries are one cell thick, and across them exchange happens: oxygen and nutrients leave, carbon dioxide and wastes enter, all down pressure gradients. Tissue fluid forms at the arterial end and mostly returns at the venous end, the exchange facts that anchor the vascular half of {S}.",
